# WhatsVault configuration — copy to `.env` and edit. NEVER commit `.env`.
#
# WhatsVault deliberately reads NO secrets from the environment. Every key and
# token lives in the macOS Keychain (see INV-ATREST in the design spec) and is
# fetched at runtime. This file therefore contains only non-secret paths and
# deployment switches. If you ever find yourself adding a token here, that is a
# bug — including the MCP bearer token and any OAuth credential.

# Root of the local data directory (databases, blobs, logs, runtime sockets).
# Default: ~/.whatsvault
WHATSVAULT_HOME=

# The https origin this server is published at, when it is published.
#
# Leave EMPTY for the normal deployment. Empty means loopback-only: a static
# bearer token, and no OAuth authorization server mounted at all — /oauth/* and
# /.well-known/* return 404.
#
# Setting it mounts an OAuth 2.1 authorization server (ChatGPT's connector
# dialog offers OAuth, No Auth or Mixed, and a static bearer fits none of them)
# and adds this hostname to the transport's Host pin. It does NOT change the
# bind address: the server still listens on 127.0.0.1 and is reached through an
# outbound tunnel. Never bind this surface publicly.
#
# Publishing means message content reaches whichever assistant you connect, on
# every tool call. Fence what should never leave first:
#   whatsvault mcp-visibility --conversation-id cnv_… --visibility LOCAL_ONLY
WHATSVAULT_PUBLIC_URL=

# The bind address and port are NOT configurable and are not read from here.
# apps/mcp/server.py pins HOST = "127.0.0.1" and PORT = 8765 as constants,
# because loopback-only binding is a design property rather than a default.
# Two variables previously documented here — WHATSVAULT_MCP_HOST and
# WHATSVAULT_MCP_PORT — were never read by any code path; setting them did
# nothing. They have been removed rather than implemented.
